1
00:02:01,941 --> 00:02:04,694
When I first moved here,

2
00:02:08,017 --> 00:02:14,720
I used to live over there:
somewhere behind the gymnasium.

3
00:02:15,071 --> 00:02:18,307
There's an inn there now, though.

4
00:02:19,923 --> 00:02:24,845
This is our original land,
and we bought that other place later.

5
00:02:24,934 --> 00:02:28,048
When the Yaeyama Islands were
being developed in this area,

6
00:02:28,122 --> 00:02:31,211
many Taiwanese people came to work.

7
00:02:31,668 --> 00:02:36,555
- Are all those graves Taiwanese?
- Some of them.

8
00:02:36,631 --> 00:02:42,055
Now it's just us, but back in the day,
there used to be a lot more Taiwanese people here.

9
00:02:42,104 --> 00:02:43,914
So that's why everyone
has their graves there?

10
00:02:43,939 --> 00:02:45,128
Yes.

11
00:02:46,593 --> 00:02:49,479
Most of them are locals' graves, though.

12
00:02:57,637 --> 00:03:04,014
Yang Huihua's family grave,
located near the bridge.

13
00:04:48,121 --> 00:04:54,738
Spirit of the Land,
please watch over my parents.

14
00:04:56,463 --> 00:04:59,633
To our ancestors,
Grandfathers and Grandmothers.

15
00:04:59,686 --> 00:05:03,690
Since I didn't bring any
fire or incense sticks today,

16
00:05:06,636 --> 00:05:10,450
I'll pay a quick visit.
I have a medical check-up tomorrow.

17
00:05:10,594 --> 00:05:13,931
My lower back hurts quickly.
I hope it gets better.

18
00:05:14,751 --> 00:05:19,429
I'll head over there soon.
See you again.

19
00:07:48,387 --> 00:07:51,614
I came here when I was 10 years old.

20
00:07:51,895 --> 00:07:57,460
Everyone called this place "Island of the Dead."
It's a place that no one wants to come to.

21
00:07:58,104 --> 00:08:02,208
Ryukyu is said to be a place that poisons people.

22
00:08:07,944 --> 00:08:11,447
A friend told me before I came here,

23
00:08:11,788 --> 00:08:16,428
"Your father is taking everyone
with the intention of killing them."

24
00:08:17,114 --> 00:08:18,699
I replied,

25
00:08:18,778 --> 00:08:21,761
"Don’t say things like that when
you haven’t even been there yet."

26
00:08:21,855 --> 00:08:25,054
I had a fight with that child over that.

27
00:08:25,302 --> 00:08:30,110
How can someone say such a frivolous thing
when they haven't even gone yet?

28
00:08:30,427 --> 00:08:35,244
The child said that Iriomote
is the island of death.

29
00:08:36,736 --> 00:08:40,292
Everyone's going to die here.

30
00:08:40,406 --> 00:08:42,622
No one wants to come.

31
00:08:44,344 --> 00:08:48,985
Most of them were single men.
They didn't bring their families with them;

32
00:08:49,112 --> 00:08:56,537
they moved around a lot
and worked in various mines.

33
00:08:56,620 --> 00:09:01,678
Some of them brought
their families with them,

34
00:09:01,748 --> 00:09:04,501
but no one else came with them.

35
00:09:12,155 --> 00:09:14,470
I didn't even go to school.

36
00:09:14,494 --> 00:09:18,164
My grandmother never even
went into the classrom.

37
00:09:19,542 --> 00:09:22,678
They called me a "coal mine thing,"

38
00:09:22,702 --> 00:09:24,871
a coal mine barbarian.

39
00:09:24,982 --> 00:09:28,107
They bullied me.

40
00:09:30,039 --> 00:09:35,512
They threw stones

41
00:09:35,742 --> 00:09:38,214
and bundles of firewood.

42
00:09:38,694 --> 00:09:41,334
They didn't reach me,

43
00:09:42,198 --> 00:09:45,494
but that's how they bullied me.

44
00:09:45,822 --> 00:09:48,324
What do they do when they go to school?

45
00:09:49,249 --> 00:09:54,337
I still don't talk to them.

46
00:10:14,114 --> 00:10:15,836
I came here in Showa 12 (1937).

47
00:10:15,989 --> 00:10:18,445
I've been here for a long time;
maybe 35 years?

48
00:10:18,602 --> 00:10:24,637
When I first arrived (in Japan),
at the Nankai Company,

49
00:10:24,834 --> 00:10:26,335
the Nankai Coal Mining Company.

50
00:10:26,563 --> 00:10:28,731
At the coal mine,

51
00:10:29,398 --> 00:10:31,867
digging coal,

52
00:10:32,048 --> 00:10:35,855
my job was to recruit workers (miners).

53
00:10:35,962 --> 00:10:38,141
I recruited over 100 (miners),

54
00:10:38,304 --> 00:10:42,655
and they were supervised
by (me,) their foreman.

55
00:11:49,042 --> 00:11:50,858
Hello!

56
00:11:50,930 --> 00:11:52,108
Hi!

57
00:11:52,179 --> 00:11:53,172
Are there any apples?

58
00:11:53,249 --> 00:11:55,632
Yes. They're already washed,
so feel free eat one.

59
00:11:55,705 --> 00:11:57,397
Okay!

60
00:11:57,470 --> 00:12:00,139
Grandma is a real "gachimaya" (glutton),
so she'll eat just about anything!

61
00:12:00,304 --> 00:12:03,012
That's right. I'm feeling well.

62
00:12:05,051 --> 00:12:06,636
But you can walk, right?

63
00:12:06,720 --> 00:12:09,973
I can walk. I'm going to walk
all the way to Sona.

64
00:12:10,060 --> 00:12:12,358
That's great!
You look full of energy!

65
00:12:13,353 --> 00:12:16,022
You know my eldest (son), Katsuo, right?

66
00:12:16,327 --> 00:12:21,531
He was born here, too,
before we took him to Taiwan.

67
00:12:21,556 --> 00:12:24,254
Well, he contracted polio:

68
00:12:24,334 --> 00:12:29,933
he came down with it that very year.

69
00:12:30,230 --> 00:12:33,763
After the (military doctor) gave him the injection,
he could no longer use his legs.

70
00:12:35,201 --> 00:12:41,475
But those aunties, or rather,
the older couples who were in Shirahama,

71
00:12:41,555 --> 00:12:44,745
- Dad and mom...
- Grandparents (adoptive) are also there.

72
00:12:44,824 --> 00:12:47,780
So, I went to Taiwan,
and then I came back to Iriomote.

73
00:12:47,875 --> 00:12:48,596
That's right.

74
00:12:48,715 --> 00:12:50,143
That's right, back and forth.

75
00:12:50,209 --> 00:12:53,343
Because grandpa was the
head of the household (patriarch),

76
00:12:53,440 --> 00:12:56,720
(my husband) was still
considered a "child."

77
00:12:57,444 --> 00:13:05,202
Kiyoshi's (third son) father
(my husband) was still quite young,

78
00:13:05,255 --> 00:13:09,442
so he always did exactly
what his parents told him.

79
00:13:10,069 --> 00:13:13,730
But grandpa and grandma
were kind, too, weren't they?

80
00:13:13,755 --> 00:13:16,790
"Come on in! Eat up, eat up!
Drink up, drink up!"

81
00:13:16,846 --> 00:13:20,581
Their hospitality was truly wonderful.
I remember that very well myself.

82
00:13:20,606 --> 00:13:24,221
I felt like I had to eat it myself, otherwise
they'd think "She thinks our food is dirty!"

83
00:13:24,246 --> 00:13:26,265
But that wasn't the case:
it was delicious.

84
00:13:26,364 --> 00:13:29,222
No, wait,
that's not really the point.

85
00:13:29,247 --> 00:13:31,113
"Does she hate (us) or something?"

86
00:13:31,221 --> 00:13:33,789
"Is that why she won't even eat our food?"

87
00:13:33,814 --> 00:13:37,797
They'd grumble that at me in Taiwanese.

88
00:13:37,911 --> 00:13:41,868
I'd say, "If she doesn't want to eat,
then don't try to force her!"

89
00:13:41,929 --> 00:13:45,492
If she keeps saying "that's gross"
over and over again.

90
00:13:45,563 --> 00:13:49,329
But it *was* delicious; I ate it all!
I was truly grateful for the meal.

91
00:14:06,447 --> 00:14:12,245
My eldest son lives in Ishigaki.

92
00:14:12,445 --> 00:14:14,781
He's crippled in one hand and one leg.

93
00:14:15,622 --> 00:14:18,845
It's his polio.

94
00:14:18,942 --> 00:14:24,531
Back then, there was
no medicine that could cure him.

95
00:14:26,747 --> 00:14:28,255
When the war ended,

96
00:14:28,307 --> 00:14:32,873
I took him to Taiwan to
visit his ancestors' graves,

97
00:14:33,123 --> 00:14:35,308
even though he was just 1 year old.

98
00:14:35,832 --> 00:14:38,091
He was just a baby.

99
00:14:47,307 --> 00:14:49,307
When he was just born,

100
00:14:50,134 --> 00:14:53,802
his hands and feet were very fleshy.

101
00:14:54,051 --> 00:14:58,954
He got that big was because he had a cold
and had to get an injection.

102
00:14:59,056 --> 00:15:04,891
The doctor was a Kuomintang military doctor
who had just arrived in Taiwan.

103
00:15:04,955 --> 00:15:08,028
What kind of drug did he inject?

104
00:15:09,380 --> 00:15:13,006
After the injection,
the child was bedridden for several days.

105
00:15:13,674 --> 00:15:16,203
Maybe the medicine was wrong.

106
00:15:17,584 --> 00:15:22,182
When he was born,
he was rated "excellent."

107
00:15:22,342 --> 00:15:28,905
Every baby would get a physical assessment:
that's how he got the "excellent" rating.

108
00:15:28,999 --> 00:15:31,738
He was judged to be "excellent."

109
00:15:31,825 --> 00:15:34,958
But ever since then...
Well, he just had bad luck.

110
00:15:35,025 --> 00:15:37,217
Because he contracted polio.

111
00:15:38,272 --> 00:15:41,635
There's no point in blaming the doctor.

112
00:15:41,702 --> 00:15:44,525
He wasn't a real doctor, though:

113
00:15:44,871 --> 00:15:49,800
He was just a military doctor who had
just returned from the battlefield.

114
00:15:51,231 --> 00:15:55,305
My son bears some responsibility, too:

115
00:15:55,388 --> 00:15:59,739
he ended up a total drunk
once he became an adult.

116
00:15:59,877 --> 00:16:02,219
Well, I guess it can't be helped.

117
00:16:34,494 --> 00:16:37,694
The kids haven't ever come home,

118
00:16:37,831 --> 00:16:40,858
though they rarely come back to begin with:

119
00:16:41,291 --> 00:16:44,171
they only visit once every few years.

120
00:16:48,959 --> 00:16:54,932
I worked hard to put my kids through school,

121
00:16:55,432 --> 00:16:59,453
yet they turned out to be unfilial,
which is most disheartening.

122
00:17:14,955 --> 00:17:21,208
What can I do?
I don't have money or skills.

123
00:17:23,326 --> 00:17:31,508
At the coal mine in Taiwan, the manager
asked my grandpa if he'd like to go to Iriomote.

124
00:17:31,651 --> 00:17:34,972
"Iriomote? I’ve never been there before!"
he replied.

125
00:17:35,004 --> 00:17:38,892
He was worried about travel expenses,
but the manager offered to cover them.

126
00:17:38,965 --> 00:17:40,597
So, we all moved here.

127
00:17:40,683 --> 00:17:45,649
I think we lived here for about 2 months?
I'm not entirely sure, though.

128
00:17:47,234 --> 00:17:50,504
I didn't think about it too much.

129
00:17:50,654 --> 00:17:55,995
When our parents said we were moving here,
could us kids really have objected?

130
00:18:46,156 --> 00:18:47,614
That bamboo shoot

131
00:18:47,727 --> 00:18:49,039
looks delicious.

132
00:18:49,064 --> 00:18:52,535
Let me have a bite,
and give me that lid, too.

133
00:18:52,633 --> 00:18:54,831
Alright, I'll take this.

134
00:18:54,892 --> 00:18:57,471
And the plate. See you later.

135
00:18:57,554 --> 00:18:58,374
Yeah.

136
00:18:58,399 --> 00:19:01,151
Haku! Get out of the way!
I'll kill you!

137
00:19:01,254 --> 00:19:02,596
Hey, get lost!

138
00:19:03,590 --> 00:19:07,231
Oh, anywhere is fine, really,
as long as you’re not in the way.

139
00:19:07,255 --> 00:19:11,151
Sorry about that. Thanks, Grandma.
I’m ready to eat!

140
00:20:08,992 --> 00:20:11,712
Grandma, do you want to sit down?

141
00:20:17,634 --> 00:20:20,844
I'm renting the room next door to an American.

142
00:20:21,151 --> 00:20:26,310
He asked me to let him stay here:
he said he had nowhere else to live.

143
00:21:19,613 --> 00:21:23,700
Peel and cook those
(bamboo shoots) right away!

144
00:21:24,351 --> 00:21:27,554
Grandma's coming!

145
00:22:42,255 --> 00:22:45,265
The original plan was to stay here
(just for a little while).

146
00:22:45,290 --> 00:22:47,861
It's small, but I love this place.

147
00:22:49,105 --> 00:22:53,663
I really like the calm vibe.

148
00:22:55,649 --> 00:23:01,171
I also feel like Grandma probably
finds it a relief having me around.

149
00:23:04,137 --> 00:23:06,613
I can drive to go shopping.

150
00:23:07,637 --> 00:23:10,330
I'm also paying rent,

151
00:23:10,415 --> 00:23:13,319
which is a struggle because
my pension is so small.

152
00:23:16,184 --> 00:23:18,017
Do you have any, Grandma?

153
00:23:18,384 --> 00:23:20,183
Yes... No,

154
00:23:20,484 --> 00:23:24,421
With a 500-yen Red Feather Community Chest,
the examination costs just 100 yen.

155
00:23:24,491 --> 00:23:26,073
600 yen.

156
00:23:26,156 --> 00:23:27,491
Without it,

157
00:23:27,548 --> 00:23:29,243
I'd be in trouble without it.

158
00:23:32,162 --> 00:23:34,188
If you don't have it, leave.

159
00:23:37,421 --> 00:23:39,709
The social expenses really add up.

160
00:23:40,420 --> 00:23:42,673
Things like entrance fees.

161
00:23:43,196 --> 00:23:49,689
Each new enrollment, you’re looking at ~12 people.
Even 2,000 yen per person, that's a lot.

162
00:23:49,763 --> 00:23:52,539
And I have to pay everyone,

163
00:23:52,692 --> 00:23:54,695
since we're neighbors.

164
00:23:54,763 --> 00:24:00,133
Everything has an established price,
which would be fine if it were written somewhere.

165
00:24:00,207 --> 00:24:02,004
Grandma, 1,000 yen?

166
00:24:03,530 --> 00:24:06,673
These days,

167
00:24:06,698 --> 00:24:10,383
if a child is born,
it's at least 3,000 yen

168
00:24:10,413 --> 00:24:12,355
that you have to give.

169
00:24:15,902 --> 00:24:19,293
Here, let me give this back to you first.
I got it from Grandma.

170
00:24:22,593 --> 00:24:26,383
Thank you very much.
I appreciate your hard work.

171
00:24:29,890 --> 00:24:32,723
- It's okay, Grandma. I'll do it myself.
- I'll stick it on for you.

172
00:24:32,747 --> 00:24:37,251
Grandma, do you have the bag for
the medicine you took this morning?

173
00:24:37,344 --> 00:24:38,375
You took it, right?

174
00:24:38,399 --> 00:24:40,247
I didn't take it.
Not yet, anyways.

175
00:24:40,921 --> 00:24:42,802
I didn't take it today yet.

176
00:24:43,416 --> 00:24:44,787
I'll take it tomorrow.

177
00:24:44,895 --> 00:24:46,346
Oh, I'll take it later.

178
00:24:46,436 --> 00:24:47,437
A mosquito is going to get in!

179
00:24:47,466 --> 00:24:49,990
It's fine, it's fine.
Alright, alright.

180
00:24:51,274 --> 00:24:53,443
- Thank you.
- Thank you very much.

181
00:25:08,214 --> 00:25:11,581
One day,
I was fourteen at the time,

182
00:25:11,606 --> 00:25:16,893
my father just announced,
"We're going to Japan!"

183
00:25:16,936 --> 00:25:20,440
"From this moment on,
we're cutting all ties with this country (USA)."

184
00:25:20,521 --> 00:25:23,980
He made it clear this
wasn't just a vacation.

185
00:25:24,100 --> 00:25:26,787
It wasn't a case of,
"We're severing ties now,

186
00:25:26,820 --> 00:25:29,863
and *then* we'll move to Japan."

187
00:25:30,063 --> 00:25:33,806
We were crossing over and <i>immediately</i>
settling down there: emigrating.

188
00:25:33,951 --> 00:25:37,384
"Sure, we don't speak the language yet,

189
00:25:37,494 --> 00:25:42,392
but once we get there,
we'll figure it out."

190
00:25:43,043 --> 00:25:45,021
So I was (still) a child at the time...

191
00:25:46,039 --> 00:25:47,844
I felt...

192
00:25:48,227 --> 00:25:55,023
If I had the opportunity
to experience other countries,

193
00:25:55,178 --> 00:25:56,543
I would definitely,

194
00:25:56,890 --> 00:25:59,142
absolutely take it.

195
00:26:00,827 --> 00:26:02,900
I'm definitely going to take it.

196
00:26:03,016 --> 00:26:04,881
So I said to my dad,

197
00:26:05,011 --> 00:26:07,417
"Alright, I'm going too!"

198
00:26:07,508 --> 00:26:09,176
Ad that’s how I came (to Japan).

199
00:26:09,846 --> 00:26:11,861
My real mother...

200
00:26:11,886 --> 00:26:14,143
She passed away,

201
00:26:14,200 --> 00:26:16,166
but she was in the Navy.

202
00:26:20,163 --> 00:26:24,407
My adoptive parents
didn't have a daughter,

203
00:26:24,857 --> 00:26:28,238
so they adopted me
right after I was born.

204
00:26:29,506 --> 00:26:34,351
They never treated me harshly.

205
00:26:35,372 --> 00:26:38,088
My adoptive father lived in
Erpingding in Xiaojilong.

206
00:26:38,182 --> 00:26:42,856
My biological father
lived in Hengshan.

207
00:26:43,020 --> 00:26:49,526
My biological father’s father and my
adoptive father’s father were talking.

208
00:26:49,689 --> 00:26:51,818
My biological grandfather said:

209
00:26:52,589 --> 00:26:58,535
"Just a few days ago, my son's wife

210
00:26:59,049 --> 00:27:00,961
gave birth to another baby girl.

211
00:27:01,054 --> 00:27:05,365
Will the baby be raised on breast milk,

212
00:27:05,389 --> 00:27:08,751
or on cow's milk?
Let's put a wager on it.

213
00:27:09,539 --> 00:27:12,249
I promise:

214
00:27:13,733 --> 00:27:17,760
if she drinks breast milk,

215
00:27:18,071 --> 00:27:24,237
adopt my granddaughter
as your adopted daughter."

216
00:27:24,684 --> 00:27:28,651
Two old men made a promise,
I was actually adopted.

217
00:27:28,748 --> 00:27:31,468
I was only a few days old.

218
00:27:35,772 --> 00:27:38,008
My adoptive father was
an uneducated man.

219
00:27:39,131 --> 00:27:42,913
But when we came to Japan,
they assigned him a secretary.

220
00:27:42,937 --> 00:27:45,932
The director treated him very well.

221
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:50,814
The director was a Japanese man
named Mr. Matsumoto.

222
00:27:51,171 --> 00:27:54,744
He was also a mine foreman.

223
00:27:54,842 --> 00:27:59,012
It was entirely up to him
to decide whom to bring in.

224
00:27:59,680 --> 00:28:04,694
Of all the miners in Taiwan, my adoptive father
was the only one chosen to go to Iriomote.

225
00:28:04,808 --> 00:28:07,614
The director only chose him.

226
00:28:08,672 --> 00:28:14,818
When my adoptive father
married my adoptive mother,

227
00:28:15,004 --> 00:28:17,314
he was growing rice and tea.

228
00:28:18,822 --> 00:28:22,870
Later, a friend came to visit.

229
00:28:22,933 --> 00:28:25,639
He said, "Hey, Soefuku..."

230
00:28:25,705 --> 00:28:28,635
My adoptive father replied, "What?"

231
00:28:28,855 --> 00:28:34,163
Friend: "Why don't you work in the coal mine?"
Adoptive Father: "What's a coal mine?"

232
00:28:34,267 --> 00:28:37,801
Back then, nobody really understood.

233
00:28:38,208 --> 00:28:40,320
Adoptive father: "What kind of place is a coal mine?"

234
00:28:40,987 --> 00:28:45,165
Friend: "You dig for coal in a
place that looks like a tunnel."

235
00:28:46,486 --> 00:28:49,823
My adoptive father replied,
"I don't want a job like that."

236
00:28:49,980 --> 00:28:56,086
But after the friend came many times,
my adoptive father decided to go.

237
00:28:56,266 --> 00:29:00,774
Even when he arrived at the mine, though,
he didn't actually dig for coal.

238
00:29:00,841 --> 00:29:04,685
Whenever he spotted a rock that
looked like it was about to fall,

239
00:29:05,162 --> 00:29:10,357
he would bring over timber to prop it up.

240
00:29:10,674 --> 00:29:14,862
That was the kind of work he did at first.

241
00:29:15,836 --> 00:29:19,352
However, after 2 months,

242
00:29:20,010 --> 00:29:25,608
he saw that everyone else was raking in cash,
but he was earning peanuts.

243
00:29:27,033 --> 00:29:30,961
This ignited a spark of ambition
in my adoptive father.

244
00:29:31,721 --> 00:29:35,692
So, partly urged by those around him,
he decided to start digging for coal.

245
00:29:35,746 --> 00:29:40,531
"Do you know what a pickaxe is?
The kind shaped like a capital T?"

246
00:29:42,168 --> 00:29:47,007
"Well, I picked one up."

247
00:29:47,521 --> 00:29:51,634
"I asked to borrow it,
saying I wanted to give it a try."

248
00:29:51,725 --> 00:29:55,779
"I told them that if I could handle the work,
I'd buy a new one to replace it."

249
00:29:56,521 --> 00:29:59,225
"So I spent 2 or 3 days digging with it,
and I said,"

250
00:29:59,970 --> 00:30:03,563
"That's it,
I've got the hang of it."

251
00:32:14,951 --> 00:32:21,881
That place where my
grandmother's house used to be,

252
00:32:24,037 --> 00:32:29,329
it seems there used to be houses
and other buildings over there.

253
00:32:29,435 --> 00:32:31,201
If you go to the river there,

254
00:32:31,278 --> 00:32:35,525
you can still find bottles
that are 50 years old.

255
00:32:35,622 --> 00:32:37,374
Surprisingly,
there are tons of them!

256
00:32:37,431 --> 00:32:39,059
I wonder why they're still there.

257
00:32:39,155 --> 00:32:42,919
I found this one in the river,
in the mud.

258
00:32:44,691 --> 00:32:48,682
It's written (the manufacture year)
that it's from the 1950s or 1940s.

259
00:32:53,794 --> 00:32:56,560
And then there's this
old-looking Fanta bottle.

260
00:32:57,941 --> 00:32:59,746
I found it in the river.

261
00:33:00,617 --> 00:33:03,423
I could clean it up more if I wanted to.

262
00:33:03,773 --> 00:33:05,068
Isn't it amazing?

263
00:33:05,922 --> 00:33:08,391
You sometimes find things like this.

264
00:33:10,574 --> 00:33:13,920
Shirahama is only 80 years old.

265
00:33:14,981 --> 00:33:16,770
It's a relatively new settlement.

266
00:33:16,847 --> 00:33:19,916
It's a town that sprang up
around the coal mines.

267
00:33:20,904 --> 00:33:26,490
There's people from the Japanese mainland,
there's people from Okinawa Main Island,

268
00:33:26,599 --> 00:33:28,014
it's a mix.

269
00:33:28,112 --> 00:33:29,179
People from Taiwan, too.

270
00:33:33,013 --> 00:33:37,498
The term "coal mine" is synonymous with death:
so many people lost their lives in them.

271
00:33:38,638 --> 00:33:42,463
Just going there...
It gives you a certain feeling.

272
00:33:43,480 --> 00:33:46,193
There are things about these places
that even I don't know...

273
00:33:46,507 --> 00:33:50,781
But I believe the stories
about both sides are true...

274
00:33:50,864 --> 00:33:57,981
I have a hunch it was on Uchirijima where
people went through the worst hardships.

275
00:33:58,121 --> 00:34:02,218
There used to be a settlement
on Uchirijima back in the day.

276
00:34:02,322 --> 00:34:05,061
There really was a settlement there;
that much is certain.

277
00:34:05,158 --> 00:34:09,633
Even today, you can still find
traces of the old rice paddies.

278
00:34:09,800 --> 00:34:11,552
The paths are still there, too.

279
00:34:12,095 --> 00:34:15,305
However, you won't find any actual
structures standing there anymore.

280
00:34:15,380 --> 00:34:20,480
On this side, right here,
no traces are left of any buildings.

281
00:34:26,233 --> 00:34:29,055
I can't go in there.
I'm not going over there.

282
00:34:29,469 --> 00:34:31,284
It's scary over there.

283
00:36:11,588 --> 00:36:14,964
I was 13 or 14 when I found out:

284
00:36:15,653 --> 00:36:18,515
I was going to become this man's wife.

285
00:36:18,919 --> 00:36:23,851
My friends, my classmates,

286
00:36:23,934 --> 00:36:26,703
would tease me about it.

287
00:36:26,794 --> 00:36:31,942
They'd ask: "Aren't you with your husband?"
I'd say: "My husband? Are you seeing ghosts?"

288
00:36:32,809 --> 00:36:35,315
Everyone around me already knew.

289
00:36:38,282 --> 00:36:41,134
I suppose that was my fate.

290
00:36:42,102 --> 00:36:47,040
It feels foolish in retrospect:
I was the only one who didn't know.

291
00:36:48,248 --> 00:36:50,243
All my adoptive mother said was,

292
00:36:50,303 --> 00:36:54,494
"From today on,
you no longer have to call him 'Brother'."

293
00:36:54,691 --> 00:36:56,550
That was all she said.

294
00:36:57,224 --> 00:37:01,842
We officially became a couple
on New Year's Eve.

295
00:37:03,891 --> 00:37:08,024
When I went to lie down
in my adoptive mother's bed,

296
00:37:08,131 --> 00:37:11,064
she told me,
"Sleep in that bed over there tonight."

297
00:37:11,678 --> 00:37:15,435
I thought it was odd.
Why *that* particular bed?

298
00:37:15,515 --> 00:37:19,880
So I laid down in that bed,
and a while later, my brother came in.

299
00:37:22,657 --> 00:37:25,201
I couldn't do anything:

300
00:37:25,519 --> 00:37:27,360
I had no choice.

301
00:37:27,674 --> 00:37:32,002
Once we grew up,
we'd get married.

302
00:37:32,058 --> 00:37:33,813
It was already decided.

303
00:37:39,847 --> 00:37:44,066
After the war,
we returned to Taiwan.

304
00:37:44,541 --> 00:37:46,850
The Japanese soldiers...

305
00:37:47,390 --> 00:37:52,723
were tormented by Chinese troops.

306
00:37:53,219 --> 00:37:55,558
It wasn't just for the
sake of tormenting them.

307
00:37:55,982 --> 00:38:01,848
During the war,
the Japanese soldiers would boast

308
00:38:01,873 --> 00:38:05,004
about how mighty Japan was,

309
00:38:05,087 --> 00:38:10,236
and they would torment Taiwanese people.
In retaliation, the Chinese...

310
00:38:10,974 --> 00:38:13,310
they tormented everyone.

311
00:38:13,961 --> 00:38:16,947
Tie them to utility poles,

312
00:38:17,277 --> 00:38:21,552
slash them with knives,
and rub salt in the wounds.

313
00:38:26,223 --> 00:38:31,711
How old was I when the war ended?
Probably in my 20s.

314
00:38:31,736 --> 00:38:34,784
I already had children by then.

315
00:38:34,901 --> 00:38:38,418
My adoptive father returned to Taiwan.
He made a living selling tires.

316
00:38:39,336 --> 00:38:41,515
But when my grandfather asked us,

317
00:38:41,598 --> 00:38:47,083
"How about we move back to Iriomote?"

318
00:38:48,111 --> 00:38:53,780
The whole family immediately agreed.

319
00:38:54,148 --> 00:38:56,686
So we secretly made our way back here.

320
00:38:56,783 --> 00:39:00,924
We waited for a boat
at Nanfang'ao, in Su'ao.

321
00:39:00,977 --> 00:39:04,511
In the postwar era,
you couldn't travel back and forth freely.

322
00:39:04,564 --> 00:39:06,513
We went through 2 sacks of rice waiting,

323
00:39:06,567 --> 00:39:08,734
yet we still couldn't get on a boat.

324
00:39:08,775 --> 00:39:13,130
There were boats there,
but they wouldn't take us on board.

325
00:39:13,155 --> 00:39:17,441
Eventually, we went to
a fisherman's house.

326
00:39:17,730 --> 00:39:21,635
We waited there for a long time.

327
00:39:21,782 --> 00:39:25,282
In the end, that man hid us away and
secretly helped us make the crossing.

328
00:39:59,356 --> 00:40:01,401
Did you already go for a swim?

329
00:40:02,705 --> 00:40:04,821
Was it fun?

330
00:40:05,345 --> 00:40:08,508
Yes, the coral was beautiful.

331
00:40:12,505 --> 00:40:14,924
Are you planning to go to the clinic?
Tomorrow?

332
00:40:14,995 --> 00:40:16,045
Yeah.

333
00:40:16,176 --> 00:40:20,180
Well, if it's not tomorrow,
even if I went today...

334
00:40:21,938 --> 00:40:24,091
Apparently, there are some
vaccinations scheduled.

335
00:40:24,171 --> 00:40:28,455
Really? So today isn't the day for that, then.
Is it a health checkup?

336
00:40:28,548 --> 00:40:29,833
I heard Shirahama
said it was tomorrow...

337
00:40:29,930 --> 00:40:33,737
Oh, right! I hard Shirahama mention
that the health checkup is tomorrow.

338
00:40:33,856 --> 00:40:35,760
- That's right!
- Yeah.

339
00:40:35,876 --> 00:40:37,664
Are you going to that?

340
00:40:39,425 --> 00:40:41,971
It's morning!
The birds are coming to wake me up.

341
00:40:42,055 --> 00:40:44,981
Oh, really?
How do they wake you?

342
00:40:45,058 --> 00:40:47,396
They go, "Coo-coo... Coo-coo..."
That sort of thing.

343
00:40:48,561 --> 00:40:52,332
If I sleep in late, they come right over.

344
00:40:52,742 --> 00:40:54,984
They sit there singing to wake me up.

345
00:40:55,078 --> 00:40:56,556
What time do you usually wake up?

346
00:40:56,613 --> 00:40:59,615
They go, "Coo-coo... Coo-coo..."

347
00:41:00,150 --> 00:41:01,778
And I say,
"Okay, I'm getting up now!"

348
00:41:01,872 --> 00:41:04,074
And then,
"Just give me a minute!"

349
00:41:09,726 --> 00:41:12,382
Well, I suppose this bird
is one of us, too.

350
00:41:12,407 --> 00:41:13,780
That's right.

351
00:41:15,642 --> 00:41:18,421
(The pronunciation of my name)
is actually "Ryoko".

352
00:41:18,928 --> 00:41:21,013
That's why I changed it to "Yoshiko" instead.

353
00:41:21,078 --> 00:41:23,400
A proper Japanese name.

354
00:41:23,890 --> 00:41:27,070
Ryoko is a Japanese name from
the Taiwan era, too, you know.

355
00:41:27,100 --> 00:41:28,361
That's true.

356
00:41:28,437 --> 00:41:33,192
You know, my...
What do you call him?

357
00:41:33,266 --> 00:41:36,219
My brother-in-law
(husband's younger brother),

358
00:41:36,580 --> 00:41:39,583
when he went to pick up his
school transfer certificate,

359
00:41:39,633 --> 00:41:43,110
they said,
"We'll put a name down for you, too."

360
00:41:43,954 --> 00:41:48,625
"For you,
we'll put down 'Ryoko'."

361
00:41:49,803 --> 00:41:51,445
My (brother-in-law) also

362
00:41:53,450 --> 00:41:57,504
changed his name after
returning (to Japan).

363
00:42:34,828 --> 00:42:38,448
My third son (Kiyoshi's older brother),

364
00:42:38,552 --> 00:42:40,093
when he went to university,

365
00:42:40,173 --> 00:42:44,354
was bullied for being
a "gaijin" (foreigner),

366
00:42:44,831 --> 00:42:48,802
so we changed our surname to "Hashima"
and became naturalized citizens.

367
00:42:50,693 --> 00:42:56,032
Later, he moved to Japan's mainland,
and we lost contact with him.

368
00:43:01,031 --> 00:43:10,174
I worked at the shop through an
introduction from my teacher.

369
00:43:10,430 --> 00:43:12,659
I actually lived there as well.

370
00:43:12,909 --> 00:43:16,363
When the shop went under,
I lost my home.

371
00:43:17,571 --> 00:43:21,654
The business didn't do well,

372
00:43:21,714 --> 00:43:25,772
so the store was seized,

373
00:43:26,550 --> 00:43:28,525
and since I was kicked out,

374
00:43:29,702 --> 00:43:31,555
so was the kid.

375
00:43:45,672 --> 00:43:49,245
To get caught in the crossfire when
the store went out of business...

376
00:43:55,068 --> 00:43:57,804
I hear it gets pretty cold in Japan,

377
00:43:57,864 --> 00:43:59,751
so, just possibly...

378
00:43:59,890 --> 00:44:02,285
he might have frozen to death.

379
00:44:05,115 --> 00:44:07,003
I can't seem to find him anywhere.

380
00:44:07,754 --> 00:44:10,484
Whether he froze to death or starved...

381
00:44:10,591 --> 00:44:13,773
I don't know what happened to him.

382
00:44:16,573 --> 00:44:19,493
I even asked my elders for help:

383
00:44:19,586 --> 00:44:23,953
"It'll be easier to find him
if you fly up into the sky."

384
00:44:31,195 --> 00:44:34,842
I searched parks and all over the place,
but he was nowhere to be found.

385
00:44:35,239 --> 00:44:40,043
I'm not sure if anyone else
is even looking for him.

386
00:44:40,120 --> 00:44:44,511
Even if there's information out there,
it might just not be reaching me.

387
00:44:45,602 --> 00:44:47,604
And so... He remains missing.

388
00:44:50,327 --> 00:44:53,193
There's no use dwelling on it, though.

389
00:44:54,241 --> 00:44:57,423
Thinking about it too much
will only ruin my health.

390
00:45:00,450 --> 00:45:02,479
Maybe it's better that
he hasn't been found,

391
00:45:02,602 --> 00:45:06,025
because the more I search,
the more painful it gets.

392
00:47:37,524 --> 00:47:42,312
We're about to start expanding the pier;
we're making it bigger.

393
00:47:43,030 --> 00:47:47,451
It doesn't really <i>need</i> to be this big,
but construction is going ahead anyways.

394
00:47:47,634 --> 00:47:52,245
The project is scheduled to wrap up
in Heisei 29 (2017).

395
00:47:52,479 --> 00:47:54,998
Since it's currently Heisei 27 (2015),

396
00:47:56,150 --> 00:48:00,243
that means it'll be finished in 2 years,
right when I turn 29.

397
00:48:01,566 --> 00:48:04,477
It doesn't really need to be <i>this</i> massive.

398
00:48:04,585 --> 00:48:06,827
Everyone thinks so.

399
00:48:31,168 --> 00:48:32,518
The neighbor's chicken...

400
00:48:47,290 --> 00:48:48,718
I'm going to beat it to death!

401
00:49:14,898 --> 00:49:16,683
The box turtle...

402
00:49:19,185 --> 00:49:23,964
crawled all the way up to my bead
and slept alongside me.

403
00:49:34,448 --> 00:49:39,739
That dog Louis owns is such a pain.

404
00:49:40,770 --> 00:49:43,483
It doesn't do anything:

405
00:49:43,583 --> 00:49:46,455
it just growls whenever
someone comes over.

406
00:49:49,890 --> 00:49:52,933
I spotted some bamboo shoots over there.

407
00:49:53,609 --> 00:49:54,634
Did you pick them?

408
00:49:54,767 --> 00:49:57,891
No, they were just sitting over there.

409
00:49:57,915 --> 00:49:58,856
Where?

410
00:49:58,939 --> 00:50:00,643
That way.
Do you think they're still good?

411
00:50:00,751 --> 00:50:02,048
Ah, no, they're not.

412
00:50:02,155 --> 00:50:03,754
Really? Even the insides?

413
00:50:03,823 --> 00:50:08,124
I'm not sure about the inside.
You might be able to salvage a bit.

414
00:50:08,168 --> 00:50:10,300
If you cut it open.

415
00:50:10,352 --> 00:50:13,567
Go ahead and cut it:
peel it and see.

416
00:50:13,680 --> 00:50:15,812
I'm fine. Do you want some?

417
00:50:17,351 --> 00:50:20,113
Alright then, maybe later.
I'm going to walk around a bit.

418
00:50:56,060 --> 00:50:58,694
- Yes, thank you.
- Yes, thank you.

419
00:51:08,533 --> 00:51:12,550
I wonder what I should do.

420
00:54:19,152 --> 00:54:20,657
Ancestors...

421
00:54:20,761 --> 00:54:23,536
Grandparents... Parents...

422
00:54:25,933 --> 00:54:30,020
Since I can't offer incense today,

423
00:54:30,103 --> 00:54:34,076
I will simply join my hands in prayer.
In its place, I offer you a cup of hot tea.

424
00:55:20,102 --> 00:55:21,110
Grandma.

425
00:55:21,187 --> 00:55:22,248
Yeah?

426
00:55:22,345 --> 00:55:23,863
Has Louis come back yet?

427
00:55:23,931 --> 00:55:25,175
Huh?

428
00:55:26,586 --> 00:55:27,310
What?

429
00:55:27,411 --> 00:55:30,273
Has Louis come back yet?

430
00:55:31,591 --> 00:55:33,636
Louis, the boy next door.

431
00:55:33,886 --> 00:55:34,714
What?

432
00:55:34,778 --> 00:55:37,260
Has Louis come back yet?

433
00:55:37,564 --> 00:55:38,982
I can't hear you!

434
00:55:41,368 --> 00:55:42,092
What did you say?

435
00:55:42,117 --> 00:55:43,937
Has your next door neighbor come back?

436
00:55:44,051 --> 00:55:44,984
He's inside.

437
00:55:45,119 --> 00:55:46,613
He's inside?

438
00:55:47,107 --> 00:55:48,491
Just one of them is occupied.

439
00:55:48,562 --> 00:55:51,511
No, wait, one of them is just where I keep
some of my adoptive mother's tools.

440
00:55:51,634 --> 00:55:53,156
No, no, not that one.

441
00:55:53,309 --> 00:55:55,549
<i>That</i> one is the American's place.

442
00:55:55,638 --> 00:55:58,731
I'm asking if the American
is actually home right now.

443
00:55:58,785 --> 00:55:59,752
Hmm... I don't know.

444
00:55:59,860 --> 00:56:01,665
Oh, I see. Alright.

445
00:56:09,019 --> 00:56:12,222
There's no point in renting
to that American guy.

446
00:56:12,322 --> 00:56:14,694
He made a total mess of the place.

447
00:56:15,329 --> 00:56:18,575
Even his hair is unkempt:

448
00:56:18,658 --> 00:56:21,488
a breeding ground for lice.

449
00:56:22,989 --> 00:56:26,342
I'm the one who ends up cleaning it:
everything, even the walls.

450
00:56:26,967 --> 00:56:29,672
Why? Because otherwise,
the ants will come in.

451
00:56:29,729 --> 00:56:34,181
Ants are constantly
crawling up the walls.

452
00:56:35,542 --> 00:56:41,047
Where did they come from?
He hauled in a mountain of futons,

453
00:56:41,965 --> 00:56:43,463
rolled them all up,

454
00:56:43,574 --> 00:56:49,860
and stuffed them into the closet
in my adoptive mother's room.

455
00:56:50,744 --> 00:56:53,529
I found them,
so I told him straight:

456
00:56:54,958 --> 00:56:58,435
"I'm not renting <i>this</i> room to you."

457
00:56:58,541 --> 00:57:00,563
"I'm only willing to rent you
that room over there."

458
00:57:00,671 --> 00:57:04,547
"This room used to belong to
my adoptive mother."

459
00:57:06,009 --> 00:57:08,702
I was furious, so I told him:

460
00:57:10,430 --> 00:57:14,214
"If you can't follow the rules,"

461
00:57:14,307 --> 00:57:16,623
"then get out!"

462
00:57:21,024 --> 00:57:22,709
Why did I rent it out?

463
00:57:22,734 --> 00:57:25,479
When I was a child,

464
00:57:25,568 --> 00:57:30,710
my adoptive father
worked in the coal mines.

465
00:57:31,361 --> 00:57:37,070
When you live in someone else's house,
how long you stay depends on the landlord.

466
00:57:37,157 --> 00:57:41,908
It reminded me of those hard times.

467
00:57:42,028 --> 00:57:44,767
Things were fine when
the landlord was in a good mood,

468
00:57:44,868 --> 00:57:48,675
but when he was in a bad mood,
he'd give such nasty looks.

469
00:57:51,115 --> 00:57:55,676
I only rented it to him because
I thought he had a wife.

470
00:57:55,755 --> 00:57:58,958
If he'd been a bachelor,
I never would have rented to him.

471
00:58:00,013 --> 00:58:03,076
But then his girlfriend's father
opposed the marriage,

472
00:58:03,409 --> 00:58:06,930
and renting to a single man
brings nothing but trouble.

473
00:58:15,162 --> 00:58:16,603
Shut up!

474
00:58:20,593 --> 00:58:21,793
You idiot.

475
00:58:31,265 --> 00:58:36,683
You're chained up in the house
all day long, just barking.

476
00:58:37,791 --> 00:58:40,103
That's why, you bastard.

477
00:59:26,022 --> 00:59:28,151
At night, when I lie down,

478
00:59:28,191 --> 00:59:32,856
I remember all sorts of things
from when I first arrived from Taiwan,

479
00:59:33,353 --> 00:59:35,692
and it keeps me awake all night long.

480
01:00:17,784 --> 01:00:21,568
When I was 18 or 19,

481
01:00:21,685 --> 01:00:25,602
a girl I was friends with said to me:

482
01:00:25,742 --> 01:00:28,015
"Let's go watch someone get beaten up."

483
01:00:28,091 --> 01:00:33,133
When I asked her why on earth
we'd watch something so vile,

484
01:00:33,420 --> 01:00:37,881
she said, "I heard a rumor about it,
so let's see if it's actually true."

485
01:00:37,970 --> 01:00:39,673
So we went.

486
01:00:39,752 --> 01:00:43,060
There he was: tied up, hanging in the air,
being beaten up mercilessly.

487
01:00:43,143 --> 01:00:47,847
I burst into tears right when I saw it.

488
01:00:51,097 --> 01:00:54,240
There are some shady people around here.

489
01:00:55,900 --> 01:01:01,713
Back in the old days,
the boss ran everything single-handedly.

490
01:01:02,118 --> 01:01:05,506
But now?
It's crawling with thugs.

491
01:01:05,592 --> 01:01:10,070
Sure, you can make money,
but the earnings (from the mine) are lousy.

492
01:01:27,554 --> 01:01:32,446
Why did they beat the laborers like that?

493
01:01:34,129 --> 01:01:39,950
It's terrifying. Those coal miners...
They were so pitiful, so miserable.

494
01:01:41,474 --> 01:01:45,806
They'd even treat people from
their own hometowns (like that).

495
01:01:46,336 --> 01:01:49,543
You know about morphine;
it's been around forever.

496
01:01:49,606 --> 01:01:53,820
You'd inject it, and then,
once a certain amount of time passed,

497
01:01:55,292 --> 01:01:57,150
you'd start shaking.

498
01:01:57,497 --> 01:01:59,503
And they'd start beating you.

499
01:02:01,147 --> 01:02:03,824
I actually saw it happen:
I saw people getting beaten.

500
01:02:08,171 --> 01:02:12,232
You'd get exhausted from the work.

501
01:02:12,348 --> 01:02:17,154
You'd lie down to sleep, but if you
took that stuff, you'd wake up in no time.

502
01:02:39,212 --> 01:02:43,577
Why am I like that?
(I get) toothaches frequently.

503
01:02:43,680 --> 01:02:46,049
It's because of the cavities.

504
01:02:47,026 --> 01:02:50,209
(After the injection,)
once I've climbed up this hill,

505
01:02:51,725 --> 01:02:54,053
the medication kicks in.

506
01:02:55,736 --> 01:02:59,402
The pain in my tooth has stopped.

507
01:03:00,397 --> 01:03:03,229
"Ah...
I can't go on like this anymore, old man."

508
01:03:05,352 --> 01:03:11,358
"If I keep doing this,
my body is going to break down."

509
01:03:11,436 --> 01:03:14,311
"No more (injections)."
"Just one, okay?"

510
01:03:14,531 --> 01:03:17,237
I try to hold out for about 3 days,

511
01:03:17,946 --> 01:03:20,741
but once the time comes,
I start trembling. It's inevitable.

512
01:03:22,258 --> 01:03:26,426
For example, right now,
it's 10... 11... 12 o'clock, right?

513
01:03:26,543 --> 01:03:29,930
As soon as this time of day hits,
my body just starts to shake.

514
01:03:36,934 --> 01:03:38,515
Even among the Taiwanese,

515
01:03:38,579 --> 01:03:42,792
surely some found the work too grueling and
tried to escape, flee to their home country, right?

516
01:03:42,929 --> 01:03:45,622
Yes, it happened. From Yonaguni,

517
01:03:46,246 --> 01:03:49,526
the (Taiwanese-run) "Hsieh-Ching Tsu":
there were many in that group.

518
01:03:50,500 --> 01:03:53,112
Some were killed, others ran away...

519
01:03:53,250 --> 01:03:58,172
There are still plenty of remains.
At the Yutsun River,

520
01:03:58,262 --> 01:04:02,729
you'll find heaps of bones in the rock (caves).
Funaura, the Yutsun River...

521
01:04:02,799 --> 01:04:08,378
Even if they managed to escape,
they'd run out of food.

522
01:04:08,468 --> 01:04:13,136
They'd use tiny mackerel fishing boats,
but they couldn't get close to shore.

523
01:04:13,213 --> 01:04:14,948
They couldn't cross to Kohama Island
(the neighboring island),

524
01:04:15,022 --> 01:04:19,058
nor to Yaeyama (Ishigaki Island),
nor to Kuroshima...

525
01:04:19,126 --> 01:04:21,414
So, many of them starved to death.

526
01:04:26,283 --> 01:04:28,481
So many died.

527
01:04:28,976 --> 01:04:31,828
Bones lay scattered over there...

528
01:04:31,931 --> 01:04:33,981
They died right there.

529
01:04:34,124 --> 01:04:36,496
And later, the people who found them

530
01:04:36,560 --> 01:04:41,331
hid them among the coral reef (rocks)
so the bones wouldn't be seen.

531
01:04:41,458 --> 01:04:47,093
It was meant as a sort of a grave,
a way to console their spirits.

532
01:04:47,141 --> 01:04:51,544
You know, after the war,
at times I slept right on top of the bones.

533
01:04:52,209 --> 01:04:56,012
I'd pick up scraps of wood,
debris from the war,

534
01:04:56,116 --> 01:04:59,059
and sleep there.
The strange thing...

535
01:04:59,833 --> 01:05:01,858
is...

536
01:05:01,975 --> 01:05:05,778
3 or 4 people were sleeping there,
we heard them actually speak.

537
01:05:05,859 --> 01:05:07,363
Everyone said so.

538
01:05:07,413 --> 01:05:08,483
The bones?

539
01:05:09,672 --> 01:05:12,815
Yes, their voices.
Was it Japanese? Taiwanese? English?

540
01:05:12,863 --> 01:05:14,314
I couldn't tell at all.

541
01:05:16,170 --> 01:05:19,319
There was nothing but the voice.

542
01:05:20,194 --> 01:05:26,093
When we were packing to leave,
we lit incense,

543
01:05:26,209 --> 01:05:31,431
slaughtered a wild boar and
roasted it (as an offering).

544
01:05:31,541 --> 01:05:35,632
It was around 4 or 5 in the morning,
before daybreak.

545
01:05:35,842 --> 01:05:39,599
A child stepped on (bones)
and cried out about it.

546
01:05:39,729 --> 01:05:42,860
It seemed as though the spirit
was trying to manifest itself.

547
01:05:43,530 --> 01:05:45,021
Since (the child stepped on bones),

548
01:05:45,112 --> 01:05:49,148
from then on,
about once or twice a month,

549
01:05:49,239 --> 01:05:52,072
we would prepare a feast and offer prayers.

550
01:06:20,981 --> 01:06:25,972
After they'd been here a long time
and saved up some money,

551
01:06:26,102 --> 01:06:29,279
they started wanting to go home,
to see their wives, children, and parents.

552
01:06:29,392 --> 01:06:31,401
It's only natural.

553
01:06:31,945 --> 01:06:34,660
But there were some people,

554
01:06:34,731 --> 01:06:39,333
to put it bluntly,
some who were nothing but low-life drifers.

555
01:06:39,943 --> 01:06:42,316
One day it's a stomach ache,

556
01:06:42,829 --> 01:06:46,483
the next it's a headache;
they wouldn't work at all.

557
01:06:46,619 --> 01:06:53,680
Yet they still ate their fill,
so their debt kept piling up.

558
01:06:53,767 --> 01:07:00,613
Then they start saying, "My father's sick,
so I have to go to back to Taiwan."

559
01:07:00,810 --> 01:07:05,552
Or their mother's sick,
or something happened to their wife.

560
01:07:05,614 --> 01:07:07,821
They wanted to go home,
but they didn't have the money.

561
01:07:07,971 --> 01:07:09,910
So we lent it to them.

562
01:07:10,030 --> 01:07:13,853
If we didn't,
they couldn't even afford a boat ticket.

563
01:07:14,491 --> 01:07:17,811
Because of things like that,
we were constantly in the red.

564
01:07:26,487 --> 01:07:32,379
The company submitted a formal petition
(to the government).

565
01:07:32,812 --> 01:07:35,162
We needed 1 chief and 1 patrolman.

566
01:07:35,298 --> 01:07:37,170
2 people (in total).

567
01:07:37,331 --> 01:07:42,980
On top of that,
we needed a separate police substation.

568
01:07:43,063 --> 01:07:46,940
These two would be assigned
specifically to protect us supervisors;

569
01:07:47,053 --> 01:07:51,854
otherwise, we couldn't do our oversight duties.
It was a struggle supervising those thugs.

570
01:07:53,158 --> 01:08:00,163
Unless I hounded him, he wouldn't work.
He was in debt, yet he just slacked off.

571
01:08:00,230 --> 01:08:03,152
He dressed the part (just a loincloth),
but he didn't actually do the job.

572
01:08:03,250 --> 01:08:07,063
It was only when I called the police
that he'd finally get back to work.

573
01:08:07,088 --> 01:08:08,785
He was a real pain to deal with.

574
01:08:08,871 --> 01:08:11,941
I suppose there were even some
yakuza-types among them.

575
01:08:13,384 --> 01:08:16,297
Basically, they wouldn't work.

576
01:08:16,377 --> 01:08:22,161
- Even though they were being fed.
- If you didn't given them food, they'd pester you.

577
01:08:22,249 --> 01:08:25,154
They wouldn't work,
just complain about everything.

578
01:08:25,536 --> 01:08:29,053
Over a debt of just a few hundred yen,

579
01:08:29,116 --> 01:08:33,481
we had to call the police
to keep things under control.

580
01:08:33,733 --> 01:08:37,654
Arresting those thugs was pointless.

581
01:08:37,730 --> 01:08:41,744
We had 2 officers come to oversee things:

582
01:08:41,872 --> 01:08:44,611
the chief and the patrolman.

583
01:08:44,911 --> 01:08:48,531
Was there a police box?
They set something like that up,

584
01:08:48,612 --> 01:08:49,963
or so I heard.

585
01:08:50,753 --> 01:08:54,888
As it turned out, (because of those thugs,)
(the mine's) profits were consistently low.

586
01:08:54,951 --> 01:08:58,579
The wages were terrible, too.

587
01:08:58,661 --> 01:09:04,257
In Taiwan, the pay was about twice as high
as (my salary) here.

588
01:09:04,394 --> 01:09:07,171
Why was that?

589
01:09:07,240 --> 01:09:10,651
Why were their wages higher than
the Japanese workers?

590
01:09:10,770 --> 01:09:12,098
Were they better at digging?

591
01:09:12,176 --> 01:09:18,568
There was a rumor...
They had to offer more to attract people.

592
01:09:18,619 --> 01:09:22,358
People heard that, once you came,
you couldn't return, so no one wanted to come.

593
01:09:22,415 --> 01:09:24,107
- You mean the Taiwanese wouldn't come?
- Right.

594
01:09:24,187 --> 01:09:30,200
The Taiwanese thought they couldn't go back.
But that was just if they were in debt.

595
01:09:30,277 --> 01:09:33,983
The mine manager asked me for a favor:

596
01:09:34,047 --> 01:09:36,093
without Taiwanese, the business would fail,

597
01:09:36,162 --> 01:09:40,046
so he wanted me to put in my contract
that I would bring Taiwanese workers over.

598
01:09:40,113 --> 01:09:44,648
The company covered travel and ship fare.

599
01:09:44,772 --> 01:09:49,833
Even though the conditions were good,
some miners were so reluctant; no clue why.

600
01:10:06,956 --> 01:10:12,752
Using Taiwanese workers made the mines
more profitable. They're very efficient.

601
01:10:12,833 --> 01:10:16,172
But they'd quickly get addicted
to morphine and the like.

602
01:10:16,209 --> 01:10:19,753
Their bones would soften...
They'd catch malaria...

603
01:10:19,833 --> 01:10:23,083
Addiction would leave them
completely incapacitated.

604
01:10:23,179 --> 01:10:24,984
They'd grow weak and fall apart.

605
01:10:25,075 --> 01:10:30,140
So many Taiwanese... They were more
efficient than Japanese and earned more.

606
01:10:30,230 --> 01:10:33,400
They'd go back (to Taiwan),
but there was no morphine there.

607
01:10:33,901 --> 01:10:36,943
No morphine, so they had
no choice but to come back here.

608
01:10:37,023 --> 01:10:39,215
- That kind of injection (morphine)...
- An injection...

609
01:10:39,323 --> 01:10:41,364
- A narcotic, right?
- It was morphine.

610
01:10:41,447 --> 01:10:46,256
They didn't have it in Taiwan,
but they did in Japan.

611
01:10:46,370 --> 01:10:50,387
If you got caught with it (in Taiwan),
you'd go straight to prison.

612
01:10:51,561 --> 01:10:54,307
After 3 years, 5 years,
they'd come back (to Iriomote).

613
01:10:54,360 --> 01:10:56,379
Because there was no morphine,
they would come back.

614
01:10:57,494 --> 01:11:02,085
They couldn't stay (in Taiwan).
They'd come back for the morphine.

615
01:11:03,013 --> 01:11:05,221
They couldn't help it.

616
01:11:07,243 --> 01:11:10,423
If they didn't use it (morphine),
they wouldn't be able to move.

617
01:11:10,677 --> 01:11:12,365
They had to use it...

618
01:11:12,413 --> 01:11:16,549
Increase the dose when earnings were high,
decrease it when earnings were low.

619
01:11:16,610 --> 01:11:19,880
The result of that constant cutting was
that they would get the shakes.

620
01:11:19,905 --> 01:11:23,566
The company held the
license for the morphine.

621
01:11:25,408 --> 01:11:27,560
1 yen for 1 shot (1 injection).

622
01:11:27,704 --> 01:11:30,874
No matter how much you earned,
it was never enough.

623
01:11:30,935 --> 01:11:33,610
Earn 2 yen, pay for (1 shot),

624
01:11:33,733 --> 01:11:35,772
and live off the (remaining) 1 yen.

625
01:11:35,963 --> 01:11:40,491
You could afford the shot for 30 sen,
but you weren't buying every single day, right?

626
01:11:41,878 --> 01:11:43,971
The boss was always on your case.

627
01:11:44,050 --> 01:11:50,644
Earn 1 yen, spend 30 sen on the shot,

628
01:11:50,757 --> 01:11:55,685
and you'd have 70 sen left.

629
01:11:55,839 --> 01:12:00,571
But then you catch malaria,
and you can't work for a few days,

630
01:12:00,647 --> 01:12:03,279
so you go back to the boss,
begging to borrow money.

631
01:12:03,393 --> 01:12:04,741
And (the boss) always lent it.

632
01:12:04,818 --> 01:12:07,310
You couldn't survive without eating,

633
01:12:07,450 --> 01:12:08,952
so the boss would lend
(the remaining balance).

634
01:12:09,065 --> 01:12:14,751
Another 10 yen in debt, 20 yen...
Eventually, you're 100 yen in debt.

635
01:12:15,292 --> 01:12:18,763
No matter how much you earned,
it was never enough to cover the debt.

636
01:12:18,890 --> 01:12:22,310
Taken right out (of your earnings),
it makes you not even want to work.

637
01:12:22,422 --> 01:12:25,386
You're lucky if you clear (a minimum of)
1 yen per day.

638
01:12:25,480 --> 01:12:28,070
You do the math, debts all over the place,

639
01:12:28,181 --> 01:12:30,300
(work) keeps stopping,

640
01:12:30,528 --> 01:12:32,404
that's just how it is for a miner.

641
01:13:14,354 --> 01:13:16,676
I've forgotten it all.

642
01:13:18,688 --> 01:13:22,302
I've forgotten everything about the past.

643
01:13:45,956 --> 01:13:51,334
The title of "boss"

644
01:13:51,431 --> 01:13:55,052
was really just a name.

645
01:13:55,893 --> 01:13:59,555
Still, when the miners came from Taiwan,
the cost of ship passage,

646
01:14:00,147 --> 01:14:03,770
we covered all the costs.

647
01:14:03,894 --> 01:14:06,069
If there were 10 miners,

648
01:14:06,113 --> 01:14:10,143
only 3 were from around here.

649
01:14:10,239 --> 01:14:12,408
There were very few locals.

650
01:14:14,244 --> 01:14:16,874
There were many Taiwanese workers.

651
01:14:16,933 --> 01:14:19,362
My adoptive father was a contractor:

652
01:14:19,442 --> 01:14:22,775
he would go to Taiwan to recruit miners.

653
01:14:25,883 --> 01:14:28,187
Since there were so many miners,

654
01:14:28,298 --> 01:14:31,538
(from the boss's perspective,)
coal mining didn't pay too well.

655
01:14:31,798 --> 01:14:33,466
It didn't pay too well.

656
01:14:36,259 --> 01:14:40,480
After the mine closed, everyone scattered.

657
01:14:41,391 --> 01:14:48,575
We gave travel money to
those returning to Taiwan.

658
01:14:48,732 --> 01:14:50,393
But some didn't go back:

659
01:14:50,490 --> 01:14:53,303
they chose to work elsewhere instead.

660
01:14:53,950 --> 01:14:56,776
We couldn't hire them for life, after all.

661
01:14:57,911 --> 01:15:02,920
Some died here; they never made it home.

662
01:15:03,003 --> 01:15:05,785
But we sent their remains back to Taiwan.

663
01:15:05,955 --> 01:15:11,338
My adopted father oversaw all of that.

664
01:15:12,412 --> 01:15:16,520
We brought all the remains back to Taiwan.

665
01:15:16,632 --> 01:15:19,770
And by "sent them home,"
I don't mean by mail:

666
01:15:19,809 --> 01:15:25,248
My adoptive father personally took them
all the way back to the port of Keelung,

667
01:15:25,976 --> 01:15:30,813
visiting each person's home one-by-one.

668
01:15:31,278 --> 01:15:35,319
They all came here of their own free will.

669
01:15:35,449 --> 01:15:40,450
I'm a woman, a little girl back then.

670
01:15:41,164 --> 01:15:49,002
I was still a child...
Sticking my nose in men's affairs,

671
01:15:49,633 --> 01:15:52,168
I couldn't do anything like that.

672
01:19:44,451 --> 01:19:49,042
I was working back then.
After work,

673
01:19:49,376 --> 01:19:54,711
I'd ride my bike back to Shirahama,
and I'd stay at my Grandma's place.

674
01:19:54,788 --> 01:19:59,088
I lived alone; that's how my days went.

675
01:19:59,209 --> 01:20:03,423
The only social connection I had was...

676
01:20:03,470 --> 01:20:06,737
hanging out with the members
of the local youth association.

677
01:20:08,097 --> 01:20:09,402
I had many worries at the time.

678
01:20:09,462 --> 01:20:16,400
I spent lots of time thinking about things,
dealing with all sorts of worries...

679
01:20:16,683 --> 01:20:23,106
Thinking so deeply was very draining.

680
01:20:23,240 --> 01:20:26,994
When you're alone,
you overthink so many things.

681
01:20:27,321 --> 01:20:28,588
The truth is,

682
01:20:28,662 --> 01:20:32,586
without even realizing it,
I rubbed a lot of people the wrong way.

683
01:20:33,834 --> 01:20:35,792
We didn't get along.

684
01:20:39,289 --> 01:20:43,663
But then, one day,
I realized I don't need to be here.

685
01:20:43,951 --> 01:20:47,971
Plus, Iriomote didn't
feel like "Japan" to me.

686
01:20:48,221 --> 01:20:50,110
It <i>is</i> Japan, but doesn't feel like Japan,

687
01:20:50,193 --> 01:20:52,179
not quite the Japan I loved.

688
01:20:52,302 --> 01:20:55,132
I missed the Japan with shrines, mountains,

689
01:20:55,665 --> 01:20:57,361
and temples...

690
01:20:58,528 --> 01:21:03,974
I wanted to go back to <i>that</i> Japan.

691
01:21:04,456 --> 01:21:06,826
Okinawa felt like a
completely different world to me.

692
01:21:07,740 --> 01:21:14,154
Once I returned to the Kansai region,
I found new goals.

693
01:21:14,291 --> 01:21:21,327
I felt determined to start fresh
and give it my all.

694
01:21:21,901 --> 01:21:27,260
But I think living that uneventful
day-to-day life is what showed me...

695
01:21:27,373 --> 01:21:33,003
what I truly needed to do.

696
01:21:36,322 --> 01:21:40,190
Nothing going on: just work, go home,
play games, have a drink, that was it.

697
01:21:40,263 --> 01:21:44,071
Thanks to those meaningless days,
dragging on like that,

698
01:21:44,194 --> 01:21:48,031
somewhere along the way,
I realized something.

699
01:23:46,269 --> 01:23:48,532
Sometimes he comes every day,

700
01:23:48,644 --> 01:23:52,322
and other times he's gone for weeks.

701
01:23:54,307 --> 01:23:58,811
He comes when he feels like walking,
and he stays away when he doesn't.

702
01:24:01,742 --> 01:24:04,828
This tombstone is different from the others.

703
01:24:05,028 --> 01:24:09,172
I couldn't bring myself to
give up the surname "Yang."

704
01:24:10,671 --> 01:24:14,721
My whole family rests here.
All my ancestors, too.

705
01:24:15,011 --> 01:24:18,044
He moved them here from Taiwan...

706
01:24:19,175 --> 01:24:23,653
about 20 years ago; it's been over 10 years
since my adoptive parents passed away.

707
01:24:27,234 --> 01:24:32,732
I was in a traffic accident back in 1979

708
01:24:32,823 --> 01:24:34,798
because of my adoptive father's nonsense.

709
01:24:34,891 --> 01:24:36,817
"I'm going to Taiwan right now

710
01:24:36,903 --> 01:24:40,080
to bring my parents' remains."
That's what he said,

711
01:24:40,203 --> 01:24:42,192
claiming he'd come back successfully.

712
01:24:42,366 --> 01:24:44,784
That's why I ended up in the accident.

713
01:24:44,867 --> 01:24:48,515
"This time, I'll finally bring back
my parents' remains.

714
01:24:49,176 --> 01:24:52,409
If I do that,
your injuries should heal faster."

715
01:24:52,549 --> 01:24:55,349
But I told him,
"Just saying it isn't enough.

716
01:24:55,512 --> 01:25:00,243
You always say that, but in the end,
you never actually bring them back.

717
01:25:00,390 --> 01:25:02,672
That's exactly why I got in this accident."

718
01:25:02,752 --> 01:25:06,523
I was discharged from the hospital
right after he left for Taiwan.

719
01:25:07,558 --> 01:25:09,860
When he came back and saw me, he asked,

720
01:25:09,943 --> 01:25:11,865
"Why did you get discharged so quickly?"

721
01:25:12,012 --> 01:25:15,192
I told him he's usually all talk,

722
01:25:15,282 --> 01:25:20,030
but this time, he actually brought
back my grandparents' remains,

723
01:25:20,120 --> 01:25:22,536
so that's why I was able to leave so quickly.

724
01:25:24,578 --> 01:25:31,034
To find my grandparents' graves,
he enlisted the help of distant relatives

725
01:25:31,131 --> 01:25:33,050
to search all over the place.

726
01:25:33,133 --> 01:25:37,240
Back then, in Taiwan,
they didn't build graves like that,

727
01:25:37,380 --> 01:25:39,929
so he didn't even know
where they were buried.

728
01:26:15,929 --> 01:26:17,914
I'll return one day, too...

729
01:26:22,108 --> 01:26:26,559
Return to the other world
to meet my ancestors.

730
01:26:32,739 --> 01:26:34,361
May I be blessed with protection.

731
01:26:34,444 --> 01:26:40,087
May my limbs move freely,
and may I manage on my own.

732
01:27:34,171 --> 01:27:39,419
I got this (bracelet) that I'm wearing

733
01:27:41,174 --> 01:27:45,001
when my grandfather was cleaning this place.

734
01:27:45,182 --> 01:27:49,005
I said, "I'll take this."

735
01:27:49,102 --> 01:27:51,561
My grandfather said "yes, yes, yes."

736
01:27:52,228 --> 01:27:56,360
My grandmother liked (the bracelet),
so she took it from me.

737
01:27:57,197 --> 01:27:59,726
It was weird...

738
01:28:01,681 --> 01:28:04,528
Why me?

739
01:28:04,768 --> 01:28:08,572
I took it off when I took a bath,

740
01:28:08,738 --> 01:28:12,192
but it wasn't there when I finished.

741
01:28:15,212 --> 01:28:22,753
I thought I'd find out if she took it.

742
01:28:23,139 --> 01:28:28,395
I visited (my adopted son)
at his home in Hiroshima.

743
01:28:30,156 --> 01:28:34,066
It was a nice place in Hiroshima.

744
01:28:37,104 --> 01:28:40,102
Well, I went to Hiroshima.

745
01:28:46,052 --> 01:28:49,416
It was sitting in front of the vanity mirror.

746
01:28:51,484 --> 01:28:56,670
It was left on the vanity, like this.

747
01:29:03,010 --> 01:29:09,249
So I called my (adopted son's) wife.
I said, "Kikue," she said, "Yes."

748
01:29:12,912 --> 01:29:15,922
"Where did this come from?"

749
01:29:16,287 --> 01:29:20,247
"I don't understand, my grandma
(the adoptive mother) sent it to me."

750
01:29:21,281 --> 01:29:24,224
Grandma to dad (adopted child).

751
01:29:26,072 --> 01:29:30,654
This is mine, so please give it to me.

752
01:29:32,145 --> 01:29:33,874
That time, I got it.

753
01:29:33,943 --> 01:29:38,118
My eldest son (grandson)
over there is looking for it.

754
01:29:38,143 --> 01:29:42,165
This belongs to grandma (me).

755
01:29:42,252 --> 01:29:44,924
I can't give this to anyone
(I can't do it).

756
01:29:44,992 --> 01:29:49,179
If grandma (me) dies,
I won't give it to anyone.

757
01:29:51,425 --> 01:29:54,311
Don't ask me to give it to you

758
01:29:54,391 --> 01:29:57,564
(or anyone else). GIve up already.

759
01:30:03,326 --> 01:30:09,840
Once in a while, I'll bury it
in the ashes of the incense burner.

760
01:30:11,978 --> 01:30:15,198
If you fill it in, the color won't change.

761
01:30:20,921 --> 01:30:23,500
It's definitely not rusty.

762
01:30:38,518 --> 01:30:44,864
<i>Flowers on a rainy night. Flowers on a rainy night.
Scattered in the storm, they fall to the ground.</i>

763
01:30:44,916 --> 01:30:51,065
<i>I see no one, and my wishes are empty.
Something that can never return to the branch.</i>

764
01:31:33,606 --> 01:31:36,215
I feel like an idiot, so to speak,

765
01:31:37,331 --> 01:31:43,253
because I only listen
to what my parents say.

766
01:32:17,737 --> 01:32:20,443
My (adopted father) and I are similar.

767
01:32:20,994 --> 01:32:24,113
That can't be true:
they're not my real parents.

768
01:32:28,421 --> 01:32:31,644
I'm not registered (Japanese citizenship),
so I'm worried I won't be able to get it.

769
01:32:31,802 --> 01:32:33,804
My (grandchildren) did it all at school.

770
01:32:33,884 --> 01:32:35,539
Nothing will happen after they graduate.

771
01:32:35,622 --> 01:32:38,792
All of my (grandchildren) are here.
They were born here.

772
01:32:38,817 --> 01:32:40,561
They don't have Taiwanese citizenship.

773
01:32:41,100 --> 01:32:42,516
They have nothing in Taiwan.

774
01:32:42,639 --> 01:32:45,482
It's natural to get (Japanese citizenship).

775
01:32:45,507 --> 01:32:47,401
I don't care if I can't get
(Japanese citizenship).

776
01:32:47,484 --> 01:32:49,676
All my grandchildren were born here.

777
01:32:49,820 --> 01:32:52,685
They have nothing to do with Taiwan.

778
01:33:07,026 --> 01:33:12,626
When the war was over,
the miners returned to Taiwan.

779
01:33:12,698 --> 01:33:16,373
I can't go back.
Nothing will happen if I go back.

780
01:33:16,503 --> 01:33:19,600
There's no house, nothing.

781
01:33:19,722 --> 01:33:21,995
I have no relatives, nothing in Taiwan.

782
01:33:22,102 --> 01:33:23,470
(I left) 35 years ago.

783
01:33:23,550 --> 01:33:26,219
After 35 years,
there's no house or anything (in Taiwan).

784
01:33:26,390 --> 01:33:30,217
I can't go back, so I just stay.

